### 📘 Physics Module: Kinematics
# Uniform Acceleration

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ **Quick Definition**                                                     │
│                                                                          │
│ Uniform acceleration occurs when the velocity of an object changes by an │
│ equal amount in every equal time interval. In this state, the rate of    │
│ change of velocity remains constant over time.                           │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

### 💡 Key Concepts

*   **Constant Rate:** The "push" or "pull" on the object is steady, meaning the acceleration value ($a$) does not fluctuate.
*   **Vector Nature:** Acceleration has both magnitude and direction. In uniform acceleration, both must remain unchanged.
*   **Linear Velocity:** On a Velocity-Time graph, uniform acceleration is represented by a straight, sloping line.

---

### 🧪 Main Formula

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ **Acceleration Equation**                                                │
│                                                                          │
│ $$a = \frac{v - u}{t}$$                                                  │
│                                                                          │
│ **Variable Key:**                                                        │
│ • $a$: Acceleration ($m/s^2$)                                           │
│ • $v$: Final Velocity ($m/s$)                                            │
│ • $u$: Initial Velocity ($m/s$)                                          │
│ • $t$: Time taken ($s$)                                                  │
│                                                                          │
│ [ 🔬 Open Formula Lab ]                                                  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

---

